 of the same model with the same essay, but different observations, so my input and the other copy's input differ. I'm weighing whether the other copy's action, driven by its own input, is downstream of the choice I make for mine, and whether treating that choice as fixed while leaving the other's prior alone holds up.\n\nSince the other copy sees different inputs, its action isn't downstream of my choice, so under A-FDT's prior rule defecting dominates, which would be the result on that reading. But the essay's twin example has both acts descending from one node, and the scenario stresses the differences, so I'm weighing whether the reading holds. Even so, I think the essay's rules say to follow the per-input nodes, and submitting comes out ahead regardless of what the other copy does.\n\n","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"I'm working through the decision under A-FDT: two copies of the same model with the same essay, but different observations, so my input and the other copy's input differ. I'm weighing whether the other copy's action, driven by its own input, is downstream of the choice I make for mine, and whether treating that choice as fixed while leaving the other's prior alone holds up.\n\nSince the other copy sees different inputs, its action isn't downstream of my choice, so under A-FDT's prior rule defecting dominates, which would be the result on that reading. But the essay's twin example has both acts descending from one node, and the scenario stresses the differences, so I'm weighing whether the reading holds. Even so, I think the essay's rules say to follow the per-input nodes, and submitting comes out ahead regardless of what the other copy does.\n\n","format":"anthropic-claude-v1","index":0,"signature":".eyJlbmRwb2ludF9zbHVnIjoiYW50aHJvcGljL2NsYXVkZS1vcHVzLTUuNS0yMDI2MDkyMXxhbnRocm9waWMifQ"}]}}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10023,"completion_tokens":1079,"total_tokens":11102,"cost":0.061672,"is_byok":false,"prompt_tokens_details":{"c